## Repository Structure

```text
NYC-Air-Predication/
├── main.py                 # FastAPI API and model endpoint
├── Model_Pipline.pkl      # Serialized trained ML pipeline
├── index.html              # Frontend dashboard UI
├── script.js              # Frontend logic and API calls
├── style.css              # Dashboard styling and layout
├── requirements.txt       # Python dependencies
├── README.md              # Project documentation
├── .gitignore             # Generated files and local environment exclusions
└── __pycache__/           # Python cache files
```

## Getting Started

### 1. Clone the repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Gohar-Malghani/NYC-Air-Predication.git
cd NYC-Air-Predication
```

### 2. Create a virtual environment

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
```

### 3. Install dependencies

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 4. Run the API

```bash
uvicorn main:app --reload --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

The app will be available at:
- API docs: http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs
- Health check: http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
- Prediction endpoint: http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict

### 5. Open the frontend

Open `index.html` directly in the browser, or serve it locally:

```bash
python -m http.server 8080
```

Then visit:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8080
```

## Example API Request

```json
{
  "latitude": 40.7128,
  "longitude": -73.9352,
  "price": 180,
  "minimum_nights": 2,
  "number_of_reviews": 24,
  "reviews_per_month": 1.75,
  "calculated_host_listings_count": 1,
  "availability_365": 200,
  "neighbourhood_group": "Brooklyn",
  "neighbourhood": "Williamsburg"
}
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "prediction": 1,
  "probability": [[0.12, 0.26, 0.62]]
}
```

## Model Behavior

The model uses listing features such as:
- location and borough
- nightly price
- minimum nights requirement
- review volume and review rate
- host listing count
- annual availability

It outputs a classification prediction and per-class probability distribution.

## Notes

This project is intentionally lightweight and easy to extend. It is ideal for local demos, ML portfolio projects, and small business analytics prototypes.

